          %title Natural numbers in SEAR
          %h1#natural_numbers_in_sear Natural numbers in SEAR
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#zero_and_one"} Zero and one
              %li
                %a{:href => "#two_and_above"} Two and above
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#alternative_definition"} Alternative definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#replacing_the_power_set_axiom_by_something_else"} Replacing the power set axiom by something else
              %li
                %a{:href => "#with_the_axiom_of_infinity"} With the axiom of infinity
              %li
                %a{:href => "#discussion"} Discussion
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            This page is a spin-off of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            described at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEAR"} SEAR
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            elements for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p The aim is to use as few axioms/results as possible, in particular, not the result that the category of SEAR sets is a topos. What we define in the first instance is along the lines of natural numbers a la von Neumann.
          %h2#zero_and_one Zero and one
          %p
            Barring opinions whether zero should be a natural number, in SEAR we have sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
            with zero and one element respectively. These follow from axioms 0, 1 and 2 at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEAR"} SEAR
          %h2#two_and_above Two and above
          %p
            To define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
            we consider
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P\mathbf{1}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
            has at least two subsets, namely
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
            be the subset of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P\mathbf{1}
            consisting of the corresponding elements. Continuing to higher numbers, we know that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{n}
            has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            elements, so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            one-element subsets. Together with another 'obvious' subset, this gives a set with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
            elements.
          %p
            More formally, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ↬
                  %mi P
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_2:\mathbf{1} \looparrowright P\mathbf{1}
            be the relation such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo ,
                  %mi empty
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_2(*,empty)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo *
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_2(*,\mathbf{1})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\phi_2|
            has two elements. Let us fix one of these and call it
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
          %p
            Now assume we have defined a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{n}
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            elements,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1,\ldots,n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 2
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P\mathbf{n}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ↬
                  %mi P
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_{n+1}:\mathbf{1} \looparrowright P\mathbf{n}
            be a relation such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo ,
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_{n+1}(*,u)
            whenever the subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ i | \epsilon(i,u)\}
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{n}
            has either exactly one element or is equal to all of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{n}
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
              \: I think it’s fine, though AN might object. A more formal thing to say would be that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo *
                    %mo ,
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_{n+1}(*,u)
              whenever the subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ i | \epsilon(i,u)\}
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{n}
              has either exactly one element or is equal to all of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{n}
            %p
              However, I don’t think your definition works when
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=1
              = succeed "!" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ̲
                      %mo =
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{1} = \mathbf{1}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo *
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(*,\mathbf{n})
              you want
              = succeed "?" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo *
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ∅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(*,\emptyset)
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"}> David Roberts
              \: I’ve fixed the problem with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_{n+1}
              and added a new definition below.
          %p
            Then a tabulation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_{n+1}
            will have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
            elements and we fix one of these as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{n+1}
          %p
            The construction on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1},\mathbf{2},\ldots
            only requires a fragment of SEAR, namely axioms 0,1,2 and 3, and even holds in the analogous fragment of bounded SEAR.
          %h3#alternative_definition Alternative definition
          %p
            To avoid having to treat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
            as a special case, we can use another definition, again starting from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{0},\mathbf{1}
            as before.
          %p
            Assume we have defined
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{n}
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ↬
                  %mi P
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_{n+1}:\mathbf{1} \looparrowright P\mathbf{n}
            be the relation such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo ,
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_{n+1}(*,u)
            whenever the subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ i | \epsilon(i,u)\}
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{n}
            has either exactly one element or no elements. A tabulation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\psi_{n+1}|
            has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
            elements, and fixing one of these we denote it by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{n+1}
          %p This definition holds in the same fragment of (bounded) SEAR as described above.
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"}> David Roberts
              \: Does this remark (about collection/power sets) belong here or in the next section? Certainly, I haven’t gotten rid of power sets and I hope I haven’t included collection.
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
              \: I think it belongs in the next section.
            %p
              %em> Toby
              \: Sorry, I put my remarks in the wrong place! It was late …
          %h2#replacing_the_power_set_axiom_by_something_else Replacing the power set axiom by something else
          %p
            As suggested by Toby, one could take as an axiom the existence of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{n}
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi …
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi …
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}\coprod(\mathbf{1} \coprod ( \ldots\coprod \mathbf{1})\ldots)
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            times). (DR: this needs spelling out better, with tabulations, but that’s the general idea).
          %p This definition also holds in the bounded fragment of SEAR.
          %h2#with_the_axiom_of_infinity With the axiom of infinity
          %blockquote
            %p
              based on
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/09/towards_a_computeraided_system.html#c027061"} this blog post
              and its predecessors
          %p
            To construe elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            as giving actual finite sets, we construct a “family”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi\colon F \to \mathbb{N}
            where each fiber
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n
            is a set of cardinality
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi m
                  %mo +
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi\colon \mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}\colon (m,n) \mapsto m + n + 1
          %p
            Then, for each
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^{-1}(n)
            is a set of cardinality
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p If you use the Axiom of Collection, then it will do this for you automatically; you don't need to come up with an ad hoc representation of the family.
          %h2#discussion Discussion
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
              \: If you don’t want to use powersets, then I’m pretty sure you’ll need an axiom of coproducts. Unless I’m mistaken, the collection of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsingleton"} subsingleton
              sets satisfies Axioms 0, 1, and 2.
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"}> David Roberts
              \: Actually what I probably mean is not use the result that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              is a topos. I think you are correct on the subsingleton front, because it looks like we have no way of obtaining sets with more than one element without more axioms.
            %p
              %em> Toby
              \: If you want to be cute, you can make the existence of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
              an axiom and then get arbitrary binary coproducts using Collection. On the other hand, if you're happy using power sets, then don't bother proving that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P\mathbf{1} = \mathbf{2}
              (which won't generalise to the intuitionistic case anyway); instead use Separation to carve out
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi P
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 1
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi ∅
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ∨
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ x \in P\mathbf{1} \;|\; x = \empty \;\vee\; x = \mathbf{1} \}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{3}
              is a subset of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P\mathbf{2}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{3}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{4}
              both subsets of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P\mathbf{2}
